  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 1 card dq reg: grade 2.3 (outside 5 to 8): "Particles are far too small and too many to count one by one. How can chemists count them?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 3 s03-dq: grade 2.3 (outside 5 to 8): "Particles are far too small and too many to count one by one. How can chemists count them?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 6 s06-rule: grade 3.4 (outside 5 to 8): "If every object has the same mass, divide the total by one object's mass. The grams cancel. A pure number is left."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 7 s07-rule: grade 5.8: "A mix of objects is counted with the average mass of one object. Average atomic masses let chemists count atoms the same way."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 8 s08-rule: grade 4.9 (outside 5 to 8): "One mole contains exactly 6.02214076 × 10 23 particles. In calculations we round it to 6.022 × 10 23. A mole is a count, not a mass."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 9 s09-rule: grade 6.5: "A mole is 6.022 × 10 23 particles. Molecules are so small that a mole of water is only about 18 g."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 10 s10-rule: grade 11.3 (outside 5 to 8): "A mole must always name its particle: atoms, molecules, formula units or ions."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 11 s11-rule: grade 4.9 (outside 5 to 8): "At one particle each second, a mole takes about 1.9 × 10 16 years. That is 1.4 million times the age of the universe. Yet a mole of water is only about 18 g."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 12 s12-rule: grade 2.6 (outside 5 to 8): "Since May 20, 2019 the number is exact: 6.02214076 × 10 23. It is no longer measured."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 13 s13-rule: grade 3.4 (outside 5 to 8): "The atoms of a crystal repeat in identical cubes. Count one cube and measure its size. Then the whole sphere is counted."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 16 s16-rule: grade 7: "Particles = moles × 6.022 × 10 23. The unit mol cancels. Always name the particle."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 17 s17-rule: grade 6.9: "Moles = particles ÷ 6.022 × 10 23. A visible sample is a small number of moles."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 18 s18-rule: grade 9.2 (outside 5 to 8): "Count metals in atoms. Count molecular compounds in molecules. Count ionic compounds in formula units."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 19 s19-rule: grade 8.9 (outside 5 to 8): "Moles of an element's atoms = moles of molecules × that element's subscript. Then multiply by 6.022 × 10 23 to count the atoms."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 21 s21-rule: grade 6.5: "A value per mole describes 6.022 × 10 23 particles. Divide by that number to get one particle."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 card prompt reg: grade 3.5 (outside 5 to 8): "One mole of copper. One mole of aluminum. A student says they must have the same mass. Explain the error."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 s23-sample: grade 4 (outside 5 to 8): "A mole is a count of 6.022 × 10 23 particles, not a mass. Both samples hold the same number of atoms. A copper atom has more mass, so the copper sample does too."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 card prompt reg: grade 3.7 (outside 5 to 8): "A student writes: 2.50 mol of sodium chloride contains 1.51 × 10 24 molecules. What is right? What is wrong?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 s24-sample: grade 4.3 (outside 5 to 8): "The number is right: 2.50 × 6.022 × 10 23 = 1.51 × 10 24. The particle is wrong. Sodium chloride has no molecules. Count formula units, each one Na + and one Cl −."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 25 card prompt reg: grade 4.9 (outside 5 to 8): "One mole of water, H 2 O, contains one mole of hydrogen atoms. True or false? Explain with the formula."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 25 s25-sample: grade 4.2 (outside 5 to 8): "False. The subscript 2 means two hydrogen atoms in each molecule. One mole of H 2 O holds 2 mol of H atoms and 1 mol of O atoms."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 26 card prompt reg dq: grade 2.3 (outside 5 to 8): "Particles are far too small and too many to count one by one. How can chemists count them?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 26 s26-sample: grade 4.5 (outside 5 to 8): "One mole is 6.022 × 10 23 named particles. A bank weighs nickels because each has the same mass. Moles × 6.022 × 10 23 gives particles. The formula's subscripts count the atoms."
